In 1905 under the guidance of Mother Evangelist Doogan, the sanctuary is beautified and a gothic altar is commissioned by[ Reid], Smart and Tappin and made by Jaan Kann[ulu]ik an Estonian craftsman. After[ Vatican] II, the altar was brought forward so that the priest performing mass could now face the congregation. Sometime after that in 1966 a decision was made to remove the central supporting pillar that divided the main chapel from the boarders' side chapel as the Sisters felt that the pillar[ obscured] the girls' view.
[Fort]y five years later, in 2010 the Chapel was suffering from extensive cracking in the sanctuary walls, which was always thought to have been caused by[ drought]. When a[ gang] of cockatoos moved into the slate roof and started to destroy it, structural engineers were called in and it was found that the structure had been put at risk by the[ pillar]'s removal. For two years the chapel was[ closed] while the[ pillar] was reinstated, the slate roof replaced and the interior, including the highly decorative paint work was completely restored.
